- The inequality holds for all scalar products on any real vector space.

- Equality holds if and only if $bold(x) = lambda bold(y)$ or $bold(y) = lambda bold(x)$ for some $lambda in RR$, that is, when the vectors are parallel.

- The geometric formula $bold(a) dot bold(b) = abs(bold(a)) abs(bold(b)) cos theta$ is well-defined because Cauchy-Schwarz ensures $-1 <= cos theta <= 1$.
